---
/**
 * Read Time tool page.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ReadTime from '../../components/tools/ReadTime';
---

<ToolLayout slug="read-time">
  <ReadTime client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      &ldquo;Five minute read&rdquo; is a word count divided by 200. The 200 has no source. It
      circulates because it is round, and it is wrong in both directions depending on what is being
      read and by whom.
    </p>
    <p>
      Spoken duration is worse, because it is not a property of the text at all. It depends on the
      voice, and speaking rate varies by more than a factor of two across the voices installed on
      one machine. No word-count model can know which voice you are about to use, so for a script
      headed for narration the estimate is a guess dressed as a number.
    </p>
    <p>
      The obvious fix is to speak the text and time it. For 500 words that is three and a half
      minutes of waiting, which is not a tool.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>Two answers, side by side, from two different methods.</p>
    <p>
      The estimate uses rates with a source. <a
        href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jml.2019.104047"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Brysbaert (2019)</a
      > meta-analysed 190 studies and 18,573 participants and found English non-fiction reads at a mean
      of 238 words per minute silently and 183 aloud. Those are the numbers here. The delivery rates below
      them are conventions from their own fields: publishers ask narrators for 150 to 160, and speech
      coaches target around 130 for a talk. Per-sentence pause time is added separately rather than folded
      into the rate, because forty short sentences take longer to read aloud than eight long ones with
      the same word count, and a pure words-per-minute model cannot express that.
    </p>
    <p>
      The measurement is the interesting half.
      <code>SpeechSynthesisUtterance</code> fires a <code>boundary</code> event as it crosses each word,
      carrying a character index and an elapsed time. So the tool starts speaking at an elevated rate
      with the volume at zero, watches the boundaries, and cancels as soon as enough have arrived to fit
      a line through. That yields characters per second <em>for that exact voice</em> in about two seconds,
      and the full duration follows by multiplication, regardless of whether the text is fifty words or
      five thousand. Press Play afterwards to hear it at any rate.
    </p>
    <p>
      One detail worth naming, because it silently breaks every implementation that does not handle
      it: <code>elapsedTime</code> is specified in seconds and Chrome has shipped it in milliseconds for
      years. Wall-clock time is the arbiter here, since a figure more than a hundred times the time actually
      spent cannot be seconds.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it stops being trustworthy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Extrapolation assumes the rest reads like the opening.</strong> True for uniform prose,
        wrong wherever the register changes, and wrong across a document that starts in paragraphs and
        ends in a table. Measuring the whole text is exact and slow; this is fast and fitted, and the
        panel says which one you are looking at.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rate scaling is treated as linear.</strong> Speaking at 2.5x and multiplying back held
        to within a few percent on the voices this was checked against. It is an approximation, and a
        lower probe rate trades speed for a little accuracy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not every browser reports progress events.</strong> Safari and several mobile engines
        have historically not fired <code>boundary</code> at all. Where they do not, the measurement refuses
        rather than inventing a figure, and the estimate is all the page can offer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A cloud voice measures the network too.</strong> Voices marked as running on the device
        are the ones to trust here. A server-side voice includes its round trip in the timing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The per-language factors are approximate.</strong> Words per minute is a poor cross-language
        unit: German compounds several English words into one, and Chinese and Japanese have no spaces
        to count by at all. For those scripts the word figure is derived from the character count and
        the measured duration is far more reliable than any word model, which is the main argument for
        measuring.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reading ease is English only.</strong> Flesch and Flesch-Kincaid are calibrated on English
        prose, and the syllable count behind them is a vowel-group heuristic that gets
        <code>queue</code> and <code>poem</code> wrong. Hidden for other languages rather than shown as
        noise.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
